<h1>Unit 23: DC Circuits</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">Circuit analysis is conservation law with a convenient vocabulary: charge conservation becomes Kirchhoff's current law, energy conservation becomes the loop law, and Ohm's law is the constitutive relation that connects them - where it is allowed to apply.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Ohm's law V = IR describes ohmic components only; a filament lamp and a diode are non-ohmic, with resistance that changes as current or temperature changes.</li><li>Kirchhoff's current law is charge conservation at a node; the loop law is energy conservation around a closed path.</li><li>Series resistances add and parallel conductances add, so series-parallel reduction is the workhorse method for solvable networks.</li><li>A source with internal resistance r delivers V = E - Ir, so its terminal voltage falls as the current it supplies rises.</li><li>Maximum power transfer to the load occurs when the load resistance equals the internal resistance, but then only half of the generated power reaches the load.</li><li>Joule heating P = I^2 R is why conductors and components get hot, and rated wattage is what limits safe operation.</li><li>Kirchhoff's laws are bookkeeping identities - charge conservation and energy conservation in disguise - so they hold for any lumped circuit regardless of the physics of the individual elements.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The lumped-circuit model assumes ideal wires and components that are small compared with the wavelength; at high frequencies transmission lines and distributed effects take over.</li><li>Kirchhoff's laws assume quasi-static behaviour, so no charge accumulates inside the wires and each node has a well-defined potential.</li><li>Ohmic behaviour is temperature-dependent, so treating R as constant is an approximation that matters in power calculations.</li><li>A real battery is not a fixed source: its terminal voltage and internal resistance both depend on state of charge, temperature and current.</li><li>Series and parallel reduction assumes ideal wires and lumped elements; at high frequency the wire's own resistance and inductance take over and the ideal rules stop holding.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Electrical power of a 60 W bulb</td><td>0.25 A at 240 V</td></tr><tr><td>Cold resistance of a 100 W, 230 V lamp</td><td>about 529 ohms</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance of 1 m of 1 mm^2 copper wire at 20 degrees C</td><td>about 0.017 ohms</td></tr><tr><td>Power dissipated in a 1 kohm resistor at 10 V</td><td>0.1 W</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>What sets the energy floor for computing with superconducting circuits?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Superconducting qubits and single-photon detectors push towards the energy-time uncertainty limit for readout, which is now the binding constraint in several architectures.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can computation be performed with less energy per operation than a transistor switch requires?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Landauer sets a thermodynamic floor and the end of Moore's-law scaling has moved the constraint into interconnect and memory energy.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Solve a two-loop circuit using Kirchhoff's laws, then verify the answer by series-parallel reduction.</li><li>Using the internal-resistance model, compare the efficiency for a load matched to the source with one that is not.</li><li>Explain from the electron's point of view why a wire carrying current is hotter than the same wire with no current.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units">NIST: SI Units</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance">OpenStax: Resistivity and Resistance</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law">OpenStax: Ohm's Law</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules">OpenStax: Kirchhoff's Rules</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
